        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.069,
            0.488,
            0.086
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part I"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.108,
            0.885,
            0.145
          ],
          "content": "For factoring $x ^ { 2 } + 8 x + 1 5$ , we need a and b such that $a + b = 8$ and $a b = 1 5$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.149,
            0.885,
            0.206
          ],
          "content": "So, $a \\; = \\; 3$ and b = 5 is one possibility. The possible length and breadth of Saira’s rectangle in terms of x are: length = x + 5 units and breadth $= x + 3$ units."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.207,
            0.656,
            0.227
          ],
          "content": "Now draw Saira’s rectangle using these pieces."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.232,
            0.885,
            0.29
          ],
          "content": "**Example 18:** A rectangular pool is such that its breadth is 4 metres less than its length and its area is 96 sq. metres. Find the length and breadth of the pool."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.29,
            0.884,
            0.328
          ],
          "content": "Let the length of the pool be x metres. Then the breadth of the pool is $x - 4$ metres."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.329,
            0.821,
            0.348
          ],
          "content": "Since the area is given to be 96 sq. metres, we get x(x – 4) = 96."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.356,
            0.352,
            0.677,
            0.371
          ],
          "content": "x^{2}-4x=96\\mathrm{~or~}x^{2}-4x-96=0.",
          "image_source": "images/page_20_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.377,
            0.884,
            0.417
          ],
          "content": "We choose appropriate factors of – 96 to split the term –4x. We know that, $(-12) \\times 8 = 96   and   (-12) + 8 = -4.$ Hence – 4x can be written as –12x + 8x ."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.423,
            0.878,
            0.442
          ],
          "content": "x^{2}-4x-96=x^{2}-12x+8x-96=x(x-12)+8(x-12)=(x-12)(x+8)=0.",
          "image_source": "images/page_20_equation_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.449,
            0.698,
            0.469
          ],
          "content": "Thus, $x^{2}-4x-96=0$ implies that $(x - 12)(x + 8) = 0$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.475,
            0.58,
            0.493
          ],
          "content": "This means either $x - 12 = 0 \\\\ 0 \\bar{x} + 8 = 0$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.499,
            0.886,
            0.56
          ],
          "content": "That is, x = 12 or x = –8. Since x is the length of the pool, it cannot be negative. Therefore, we ignore x = –8 and x = 12 metres must be the length of the pool. The breadth of the pool $x - 4 = 12 - 4 = 8$ metres."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.301,
            0.585,
            0.692,
            0.613
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "End-of-Chapter Exercises"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.169,
            0.633,
            0.763,
            0.87
          ],
          "content": "Use suitable identities to find the following products: (i) $(-3x + 4)^{2}$ (ii) $\\left( 2 s + 7 \\right) \\left( 2 s - 7 \\right)$ (iii) $\\left(p^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(p^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ (iv) $( 2 n + 7 )   ( 2 n - 7 )$ (v) $(s - 2t)\\left(s^{2} + 2st + 4t^{2}\\right)$ (vi) $\\left( { \\frac { 1 } { 2 r } }   -   4 r \\right) ^ { 2 }$ (vii) $( - 3 m + 4 k - l ) ^ { 2 }$ (viii) $\\left( x   -   { \\frac { 1 } { 3 } } y \\right) ^ { 3 }$ (ix) $\\left( { \\frac { 7 } { 2 } } k   -   { \\frac { 2 } { 3 } } m \\right) ^ { 3 }$"
        }
      ]
    }
